n't necessarily mean the rabbit is visiting the bald eagle.\n\nSo, putting it all together: The rabbit is visiting the lion (14), which means the rabbit needs the bald eagle (20). But because the lion doesn't need the bald eagle, statement 16 doesn't force the rabbit to visit the bald eagle. Therefore, the rabbit doesn't visit the bald eagle.\n\nBut wait, the question is whether the rabbit does not see the bald eagle. So, if the rabbit doesn't visit the bald eagle, then the statement \"The rabbit does not see the bald eagle\" would be true.\n\nWait, but I'm not sure if \"visits\" and \"sees\" are the same thing. The problem uses \"visits\" in some statements, but the question is about \"seeing.\" Maybe \"visits\" implies seeing, but I'm not certain. However, given the context, I think \"visits\" and \"seeing\" are interchangeable here.\n\nSo, if the rabbit doesn't visit the bald eagle, then the statement \"The rabbit does not see the bald eagle\" is true.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that the rabbit needs the bald eagle because it's visiting the lion, which leads to needing the bald eagle. But statement 16 only applies if the something (lion) needs the bald eagle, which it doesn't. So, the rabbit doesn't have to visit the bald eagle.\n\nTherefore, the rabbit does not visit the bald eagle, so the statement \"The rabbit does not see the bald eagle\" is true.\n\nWait, but I'm getting confused because statement 4 says the bald eagle visits the rabbit, which might imply that the rabbit is being visited by the bald eagle, but that doesn't necessarily mean the rabbit is visiting the bald eagle.\n\nSo, to clarify: The bald eagle visits the rabbit (4), meaning the bald eagle is visiting the rabbit, but that doesn't mean the rabbit is visiting the bald eagle. Therefore, the rabbit doesn't visit the bald eagle, so the statement \"The rabbit does not see the bald eagle\" is true.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that because the rabbit is visiting the lion, it needs the bald eagle, but statement 16 only applies if the something (lion) needs the bald eagle, which it doesn't. So, the rabbit doesn't have to visit the bald eagle.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be that the statement is true: The rabbit does not see the bald eagle.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"The rabbit does not see the bald eagle\" is true because the rabbit is visiting the lion, which doesn't require the bald eagle, so the rabbit doesn't visit the bald eagle.\n\nAnswer: A) True",
