 is round.\n14.If something is round and it visits the dog, then it chases the tiger.\n15.If something is big, then it visits the rabbit.\n\nWait, hold on.Point 7 says "The tiger is green."So according to that, the tiger is green.But the question is asking if the tiger is not green.So if the tiger is green, then the statement "The tiger is not green" would be false.\n\nBut let me make sure I\'m not missing anything.Maybe there\'s a contradiction or something else that could change this conclusion.\n\nLooking at point 3: "The mouse is green."So the mouse is green.Point 9 says if something eats the tiger, then the tiger visits the mouse.But point 2 says the mouse eats the tiger, so that would mean the tiger visits the mouse.But point 7 already states the tiger is green, so that\'s consistent.\n\nPoint 12 says if something is green, it visits the dog.Since the tiger is green (point 7), the tiger would visit the dog.But point 15 says if something is big, it visits the rabbit.I don\'t know if the tiger is big yet.\n\nPoint 14: If something is round and visits the dog, it chases the tiger.But I don\'t know if the tiger is round.\n\nThe rabbit is not green, the tiger is not chasing the mouse, and the dog does not eat the rabbit, which means the rabbit is not round and the mouse is not big.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
