messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe cat chases the dog. The cat chases the mouse. The cat chases the tiger. The cat eats the dog. The dog is big. The dog sees the cat. The mouse does not eat the cat. The mouse eats the tiger. The mouse does not see the tiger. The tiger eats the dog. The tiger is nice. If something is young and it eats the tiger then it eats the dog. If something sees the mouse and it eats the mouse then the mouse sees the tiger. If something sees the mouse then it is big. If something chases the dog then the dog chases the cat. If something chases the dog then it chases the cat. If something chases the tiger and the tiger eats the cat then the cat sees the mouse. If something sees the dog and it chases the tiger then the tiger chases the dog. If the tiger is green and the tiger is big then the tiger sees the dog. If something chases the cat then it sees the dog.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The tiger sees the dog.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The tiger sees the dog" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given statements to make it easier to reference:.\n\n1.The cat chases the dog.\n2.The cat chases the mouse.\n3.The cat chases the tiger.\n4.The cat eats the dog.\n5.The dog is big.\n6.The dog sees the cat.\n7.The mouse does not eat the cat.\n8.The mouse eats the tiger.\n9.The mouse does not see the tiger.\n10.The tiger eats the dog.\n11.The tiger is nice.\n12.If something is young and it eats the tiger, then it eats the dog.\n13.If something sees the mouse and it eats the mouse, then the mouse sees the tiger.\n14.If something sees the mouse, then it is big.\n15.If something chases the dog, then the dog chases the cat.\n16.If something chases the dog, then it chases the cat.\n17.If something chases the tiger and the tiger eats the cat, then the cat sees the mouse.\n18.If something sees the dog and it chases the tiger, then the tiger chases the dog.\n19.If the tiger is green and the tiger is big, then the tiger sees the dog.\n20.If something chases the cat, then it sees the dog.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the tiger sees the dog.Let\'s see what we know.\n\nFrom statement 10, the tiger eats the dog.From statement 19, if the tiger is green and big, then it sees the dog.But from statement 5, the dog is big.So the tiger is big, but we don\'t know if the tiger is green.Statement 11 says the tiger is nice, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean it\'s green.\n\nLooking at statement 12: If something is young and eats the tiger, it eats the dog.But we don\'t know if the tiger is young or not.The context doesn\'t mention the tiger\'s age, so we can\'t use this rule.\n\nStatement 18: If something sees the dog and chases the tiger, then the tiger chases the dog.But we don\'t know if the tiger is chasing the dog or if the tiger sees the dog.So this doesn\'t directly help.\n\nStatement 17: If something chases the tiger and the tiger eats the cat, then the cat sees the mouse.But the cat is chasing the tiger (statement 3), and the tiger eats the dog (statement 10), not the cat.So this condition isn\'t met.\n\nStatement 20: If the tiger is green and big, it sees the dog.We know the tiger is big, but we don\'t know if it\'s green.So this rule could apply if the tiger is green, but we don\'t have information about that.\n\nLooking at statement 14: If something sees the mouse, then it is big.The mouse does not see the tiger (statement 9), so the mouse isn\'t seeing the tiger.But the mouse eats the tiger (statement 8), so the mouse is eating the tiger.However, statement 7 says the mouse does not eat the cat, which is consistent.\n\nFrom statement 6, the dog sees the cat (statement 6).From statement 1, the cat chases the dog, and from statement 15 and 16, if the cat chases the dog, then the dog chases the cat.So they chase each other.\nThe tiger doesn\'t see the dog because the tiger is big and the dog is big.But statement 19 is the only rule that disconnects seeing the dog from the tiger\'s color.With knowing the tiger\'s color, we can conclude.. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 27%|##6       | 160/600 [27:48<29:59,  4.09s/it]
