from somewhere) and visits the mouse, so the mouse needs the rabbit.\n\nBut where does the rabbit needing the mouse come from?Statement 12 says the squirrel needs the mouse, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean the rabbit needs the mouse.Unless there\'s a rule that if the squirrel needs the mouse, then the rabbit also needs the mouse, but I don\'t see that in the given statements.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m missing something.Let me check the statements again.\n\nStatement 12: The squirrel needs the mouse.So, the squirrel requires the mouse.But does that mean the mouse is needed by the squirrel, or that the mouse needs the squirrel?It\'s a bit ambiguous, but in logical terms, "needs" could mean that the mouse is required by the squirrel, i.e., the squirrel requires the mouse, but not necessarily that the mouse requires the squirrel.\n\nSo, if the rabbit needs the mouse, then when the rabbit visits the mouse, the mouse needs the rabbit.But does the rabbit need the mouse?Statement 12 says the squirrel needs the mouse, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean the rabbit needs the mouse.Unless there\'s a rule that if the squirrel needs the mouse, then the rabbit also needs the mouse, but I don\'t see that in the given statements.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m misunderstanding.Let me think differently.Maybe the fact that the squirrel needs the mouse implies that the mouse is needed by the squirrel, but the rabbit\'s need for the mouse isn\'t directly stated.So, unless the rabbit is required by the squirrel, which isn\'t stated, we can\'t assume the rabbit needs the mouse.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit\'s roughness implies it visits the mouse, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean the rabbit needs the mouse.It just means the rabbit visits the mouse.So, unless the rabbit needs the mouse, statement 17 doesn\'t apply.\n\nWait, but statement 17 says: If something needs the rabbit and it visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the rabbit.So, the antecedent is "something needs the rabbit and it visits 