hite.But wait, point 10 says Gary is red.So, Gary is both red and white?That seems contradictory because if being cold implies being white, and Gary is cold, he should be white.But he\'s also red.Hmm, that\'s a conflict.Let me check if there\'s any rule that connects color and being smart or something else.\n\nPoint 17 says all young, red people are smart.So, if someone is young and red, they are smart.But Gary is red, but we don\'t know if he\'s young yet.Wait, point 14 says if someone is white, then they are young.So, since Gary is white (from point 15 because he\'s cold), he must be young.So, Gary is young and red, which makes him smart according to point 17.\n\nWait, but let me make sure.Let\'s go through each point again.\n\nGary is cold (9) → cold implies white (15) → so Gary is white.Gary is not smart; however, Fiona is red, young, and not nice. Anne is not smart, cold, or white. Dave is not smart, green, or nice. Gary is not red, young, or white. Fiona is not red, young, or smart. Anne is not red, green, or nice. Dave is not red, green, or young. Fiona is not green, smart, or white. Anne is not green, smart, or white. Dave is not red, young, or nice. Therefore, Gary is not smart because he is not red, young, or white.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
