sits the cow.\n18.If something likes the tiger and the tiger is big, then it is big.\n19.If something visits the cow and the cow is young, then it is big.\n20.If something visits the tiger, then it is young.\n21.If the tiger likes the dog, then the tiger visits the bald eagle.\n22.If something is big, then it visits the dog.\n23.If something visits the dog, then it is cold.\n24.If something is young, then it likes the tiger.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the dog is young.So, I need to see if we can conclude that the dog is young based on these statements.\n\nLet me start by looking at the dog\'s interactions.The dog sees the bald eagle (statement 11), and the dog visits the tiger (statement 12).Also, the tiger likes the dog?Wait, no, statement 21 says if the tiger likes the dog, then the tiger visits the bald eagle.But we don\'t know if the tiger likes the dog.So, that\'s a conditional statement, but we don\'t have evidence that the tiger likes the dog.\n\nLooking at statement 24: If something is young, then it likes the tiger.So, if the dog is young, then it likes the tiger.But we don\'t know if the dog is young yet.\n\nStatement 19: If something visits the cow and the cow is young, then it is big.The cow is young (statement 9), so if something visits the cow, then that something is big.The dog is old.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
