messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe bald eagle eats the cow. The bald eagle does not eat the dog. The bald eagle is rough. The cow is round. The cow sees the bald eagle. The cow does not see the dog. The cow visits the bald eagle. The cow visits the lion. The dog is rough. The lion is young. The lion does not see the bald eagle. The lion sees the cow. If someone is green and they eat the bald eagle then the bald eagle is not rough. If someone is big and they do not see the bald eagle then the bald eagle is rough. If someone is big then they visit the dog. If someone eats the lion and they are big then the lion eats the dog. If someone visits the dog then the dog eats the cow. If someone is rough and they eat the cow then they are young. If the lion eats the cow then the lion visits the bald eagle. If someone is big and they see the lion then they are green. If someone is young then they are big.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The lion is round.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The lion is round" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given facts and rules to make sure I don\'t miss anything.\n\n1.The bald eagle eats the cow.\n2.The bald eagle does not eat the dog.\n3.The bald eagle is rough.\n4.The cow is round.\n5.The cow sees the bald eagle.\n6.The cow does not see the dog.\n7.The cow visits the bald eagle.\n8.The cow visits the lion.\n9.The dog is rough.\n10.The lion is young.\n11.The lion does not see the bald eagle.\n12.The lion sees the cow.\n13.If someone is green and they eat the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is not rough.\n14.If someone is big and they do not see the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is rough.\n15.If someone is big, then they visit the dog.\n16.If someone eats the lion and they are big, then the lion eats the dog.\n17.If someone visits the dog, then the dog eats the cow.\n18.If someone is rough and they eat the cow, then they are young.\n19.If the lion eats the cow, then the lion visits the bald eagle.\n20.If someone is big and they see the lion, then they are green.\n21.If someone is young, then they are big.\n\nNow, I need to determine if the lion is round.Let\'s see what we know about the lion.\n\nFrom point 10, the lion is young.From point 21, if someone is young, then they are big.So the lion is both young and big.\n\nFrom point 12, the lion does not see the bald eagle.From point 14, if someone is big and does not see the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is rough.Since the lion is big and does not see the bald eagle, this condition applies, and thus the bald eagle is rough.But wait, from point 3, the bald eagle is already stated to be rough.So that\'s consistent.\n\nFrom point 19, if the lion eats the cow, then the lion visits the bald eagle.But we don\'t know if the lion eats the cow yet.Let\'s see if that\'s possible.\n\nFrom point 8, the cow visits the lion.From point 17, if someone visits the dog, then the dog eats the cow.So if the cow visits the lion, does that affect whether the dog eats the cow?Wait, no, because the cow is visiting the lion, not the dog.So the fact that the cow visits the lion doesn\'t directly affect whether the dog eats the cow.But from point 6, the cow does not see the dog.So the cow doesn\'t see the dog, but from point 17, if someone visits the dog, the dog eats the cow.Since the cow doesn\'t visit the dog, that doesn\'t necessarily mean the dog doesn\'t eat the cow.It\'s possible that the dog does or doesn\'t, but we don\'t have enough info.\n\nWait, but from point 16, if someone eats the lion and is big, then the lion eats the dog.The lion is big, so if the lion eats the lion (which is itself), that would make the lion eat the dog.But the lion can\'t eat itself, so that\'s impossible.Therefore, the lion doesn\'t eat the lion, so the condition in point 16 doesn\'t apply.So the lion doesn\'t eat the dog.\n\nFrom point 17, if someone visits the dog, the dog eats the cow.The cow doesn\'t visit the dog, so that doesn\'t affect whether the dog eats the cow.But from point 8, the cow visits the lion, and from point 12, the lion sees the cow.Wait, point 12 says the lion sees the cow, but point 8 says the cow visits the lion.So the cow is visiting the lion, which means the cow sees the lion.But from point 12, the lion sees the cow, which is the same as the cow seeing the lion.So that\'s consistent.\n\nBased on the available information, it is clear that the lion is not round.. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 49%|####8     | 293/600 [50:48<10:43,  2.10s/it]
