ions that affect the conclusion.\nIn fact, Harry is not big at all. In addition, Harry is also green according to Fact 5, which directly impacts his color. Looking at the implications: - From fact 9: If someone is quiet, they are not smart. Harry is quiet, so he must not be smart. - From fact 10: If Dave is red, then Dave is not big. Dave is red (fact 1), so that\'s inconsistent with Dave being big (fact 4). - From fact 11: If Dave is smart and white, then Dave is not big. But Dave is red, not white, so this condition applies to Dave. - Fact 12: If Fiona is white and red, then she\'s not rough. But Fiona is white (fact 3) and red (fact 13, which says white people are not quiet, but Fiona is white, so she\'s not quiet, but the color red isn\'t specified for her. Wait, fact 13 says if someone is white, they are not quiet, but it doesn\'t say anything about their color. So Fiona is white, but her color isn\'t specified. However, fact 3 says Fiona is white, but fact 12 talks about Fiona being white and red, which isn\'t the case because Fiona is only white. So fact 12 applies to Fiona. Back to Harry. Fact 7 says Harry is not quiet, so from fact 9, he\'s not smart. Then, looking at fact 14: rough people are not green. But Harry is green (fact 5), so if Harry were not rough, he\'d have to be green, which he is. Therefore, Harry is rough. But wait, does Harry not being red affect this? From fact 8, Harry is not red. So he\'s not red, quiet, smart, big, and green. The only thing that could make Harry red is if there\'s a contradiction, but fact 8 clearly states he\'s not red. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
