he tiger.Hmm, conflicting again.\n\nWait, statement 3: The bald eagle sees the tiger.So the eagle is seeing the tiger, but does that mean the tiger is seeing the eagle?Not necessarily.So the tiger might not see the eagle.But statement 6 says the bear sees the tiger, and statement 7 says the bear does not see the tiger.That\'s a contradiction.Maybe one of them is wrong, but I have to work with it.\n\nLet me try to see if I can find a way through..\n\nFrom statement 17: If the bald eagle visits the bear and the bald eagle needs the tiger, then the bear needs the tiger.The bald eagle does visit the bear (statement 4), and the eagle needs the tiger (from statement 14).So according to statement 17, the bear needs the tiger.But from statement 5, the bear already needs the eagle.So the bear needs both the eagle and the tiger.\n\nFrom statement 18: If something needs the tiger, then it is red.The bear needs the tiger (from above), so the bear must be red.But statement 2 says the eagle is red.So the bear is red.\n\nFrom statement 19: If the bear sees the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the tiger.So if the bear sees the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the tiger.But I don\'t know yet if the bear sees the rabbit.\n\nFrom statement 20: If something is blue and needs the tiger, then it does not visit the eagle.I don\'t know if the bear is blue yet, but from statement 18, the bear is red, so it\'s not blue.So this statement doesn\'t affect the bear.\n\nFrom statement 21: If something is blue and needs the tiger, it does not visit the eagle.Again, the bear is red, so it\'s not blue, so this doesn\'t apply.\n\nNow, let\'s see about the bear visiting the rabbit.The question is whether the bear does not visit the rabbit, i.e., is the statement "The bear does not visit the rabbit" true, false, or unknown.\n\nFrom statement 19: If the bear sees the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the tiger.But I don\'t know if the bear sees the rabbit.So I need to find out if the bear sees the rabbit.\n\nFrom statement 10: The rabbit needs the bear.So the bear is needed by the rabbit.From statement 12: The tiger needs the rabbit.So the rabbit is needed by the tiger.From statement 13: The tiger visits the bear.So the tiger goes to the bear.From statement 17: The bear needs the tiger because the eagle visits the bear and the eagle needs the tiger.\n\nNow, from statement 19: If the bear sees the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the tiger.But the rabbit already needs the tiger (statement 12), so maybe that\'s consistent.But does the bear see the rabbit?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear does not see the rabbit, which would make the statement "The bear does not visit the rabbit" true.But I\'m not sure.\n\nWait, let\'s think about the implications.The rabbit needs the bear (statement 10) and the tiger needs the rabbit (statement 12).So the rabbit is needed by both the bear and the tiger.The tiger visits the bear (statement 13), so the tiger goes to the bear.The bear needs the tiger (from statement 17), so the bear is visited by the tiger.\n\nNow, if the bear sees the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the tiger (statement 19).But the rabbit already needs the tiger, so that\'s possible.But does the bear see the rabbit?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear does not see the rabbit, which would make the statement "The bear does not visit the rabbit" true.But I\'m not sure if that\'s necessarily the case.\n\nWait, let\'s look at statement 19 again: If the bear sees the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the tiger.But the rabbit already needs the tiger, so maybe the rabbit sees the tiger regardless.The bear does not see the rabbit because the rabbit is being visited by the tiger, which is being visited by the bear.So the bear could be seeing the rabbit through the tiger.But that\'s speculative.. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 61%|######    | 364/600 [1:00:16<04:09,  1.06s/it]
