on.So maybe there\'s an error in the logic here.\n\nWait, perhaps I\'m misapplying the statements.Let me try again.\n\nStatement 22: If something sees the cat and the cat visits the lion, then it is not rough.\n\nSo, if the rabbit sees the cat (which it does, statement 16) and the cat visits the lion (statement 11), then the rabbit is not rough.But statement 14 says the rabbit is rough.So that\'s a contradiction.That suggests that the rabbit cannot be rough, but it is rough.Therefore, this is impossible.So perhaps the rabbit is not rough, but statement 14 says it is.That\'s a problem.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m misunderstanding the direction of the implications.Let me check statement 22 again.It says "if something sees the cat and the cat visits the lion, then it is not rough."So, if both conditions are met, then the something is not rough.But in our case, the rabbit sees the cat, and the cat visits the lion, so the rabbit is not rough.But statement 14 says the rabbit is rough.So that\'s a contradiction.Therefore, the rabbit cannot be rough, but it is.So that\'s impossible.Therefore, perhaps the rabbit is not rough, but statement 14 says it is.So that\'s a problem.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m misapplying the statements.Let me check statement 14 again: The rabbit is rough.So that\'s a fact.Then statement 22 says that if the rabbit sees the cat and the cat visits the lion, then the rabbit is not rough.But since the rabbit is rough, that would mean that the condition in statement 22 is false.So, either the rabbit does not see the cat, or the cat does not visit the lion.But according to the given statements, the rabbit does see the cat (statement 16) and the cat does visit the lion (statement 11).Therefore, statement 22 would imply that the rabbit is not rough, which contradicts statement 14.So that\'s a problem.\n\nThis suggests that there\'s an inconsistency in the given statements, which is confusing.Maybe I\'m missing something.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rabbit does not visit 