ternatively, maybe there\'s a way to deduce her color.Let\'s see: Alice is red and square.All unkind things are not green.So if Alice is unkind, she\'s not green.But we don\'t know if Alice is unkind.John is warm and unkind, so he\'s unkind.But that doesn\'t impact Erin.\n\nOh, but if not all unkind things are green, and John is unkind, then John is not green.But John is warm.So John is warm and unkind, which makes him square.But Alice is red and square.So maybe being square doesn\'t necessarily mean anything about color, except that unkind things are not green.\n\nBut Erin is smooth, so she\'s square and warm.If she\'s intelligent, she\'s unkind and not green.But we do know she\'s intelligent.So the only thing we can say is that if she\'s intelligent, she\'s not green.But since we know her color, we can confirm.\n\nOh, but consider this: not all unkind things are green.So if Erin is unkind, she\'s not green.But we do know she\'s not unkind.So perhaps she\'s kind, which would imply she\'s smart.But that\'s factual.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a way to deduce her color.Let\'s see: If all unkind things are not green, and John is unkind, then John is not green.But Alice is red and square.So maybe being square doesn\'t necessarily mean anything about color, except that unkind things are not green.\n\nBut Erin is smooth, so she\'s square and warm.If she\'s intelligent, she\'s unkind and not green.But we do know she\'s intelligent.So the only thing we can say is that if she\'s intelligent, she\'s not green.But since we know her color, we can confirm.\n\nOh, but consider this: not all unkind things are green.So if Erin is unkind, she\'s not green.But we do know she\'s not unkind.So perhaps she\'s kind, which would imply she\'s smart.But that\'s factual.\n\nI believe the key is that we have plenty of evidence directly connecting Erin\'s color to her intelligence.The clear link is that if she\'s intelligent, she\'s unkind and not green.However, as we do know her color, 