g is big, it eats the cat.\n20.If nothing eats the rabbit, then the rabbit doesn\'t eat the dog.\n\nNow, the answer is whether the cat is green.So, I need to establish if the cat\'s color is possibly green, or if it\'s certain based on the given information.\n\nLet\'s begin by examining the cat\'s characteristics.From statement 2, the cat is not young.From statement 10, the rabbit doesn\'t eat the cat.From statement 15, if nothing eats the cat, then the cat is not green.So, as the rabbit doesn\'t eat the cat, as per statement 15, the cat cannot be green.\n\nHold on, this seems complex.So, the cat is not green because the rabbit doesn\'t eat it, and as per statement 15, that indicates the cat is not green.Therefore, the assertion "The cat is not green" would be true as the cat is not green.\n\nHowever, let me reevaluate to ensure I\'m not overlooking anything.Let\'s revisit the pertinent statements.\n\nStatement 10: The rabbit doesn\'t eat the cat.\n\nStatement 15: If nothing eats the cat, then the cat is not green.\n\nTherefore, if the rabbit doesn\'t eat the cat, then the cat is not green.Thus, the cat is not green.Consequently, the statement "The cat is not green" is true.\n\nI don\'t perceive any inconsistencies or alternate possibilities here.The cat\'s color is directly determined by the fact that it\'s not eaten by the rabbit, which fulfills the condition that makes it not green.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
