abbit.\n\nSo, the bear is cold but we don\'t know if it\'s nice.The rabbit is cold, but we don\'t know if it\'s nice.The tiger is cold and nice (rules 10 and 11).\n\nSo, for rule 18, if something is cold and not nice, it eats the rabbit.\n\nSo, if the bear is cold and not nice, it would eat the rabbit.But we don\'t know if the bear is nice.Rule 2 says the bear is not green, but nothing about being nice.\n\nSimilarly, the rabbit is cold, but we don\'t know if it\'s nice.If the rabbit is not nice, then the rabbit being cold and not nice would make it eat the rabbit, which doesn\'t make sense.So, maybe the rabbit is nice.\n\nThe squirrel is nice (rule 5), but the squirrel doesn\'t visit the rabbit (rule 6).So, the rabbit\'s niceness isn\'t specified, but perhaps it\'s nice or not.\n\nNow, let\'s see if the rabbit is nice or not.If the rabbit is nice, then rule 18 doesn\'t apply to it because it\'s not cold and not nice.If the rabbit is not nice, then since it\'s cold, it would eat the rabbit, which is a bit of a loop.\n\nBut let\'s see if we can find out more.\n\nLooking at rule 19: If something eats the bear and the bear visits the squirrel, then it eats the rabbit.\n\nSo, if X eats the bear, and the bear visits the squirrel, then X eats the rabbit.\n\nBut does the bear visit the squirrel?Rule 7 says the squirrel visits the tiger, and rule 14 says the tiger visits the squirrel.So, the squirrel visits the tiger, and the tiger visits the squirrel.So, it\'s a mutual visit.But does the bear visit the squirrel?Rule 6 says the squirrel doesn\'t visit the rabbit, but nothing about the bear.\n\nWait, rule 17: If the squirrel eats the rabbit and the rabbit visits the bear, then the bear is not red.\n\nBut we don\'t know if the squirrel eats the rabbit.Rule 9 says the tiger does not eat the rabbit (rule 9).Rule 12 says the tiger is not rough.Rule 13 says the tiger visits the rabbit.So, the tiger visits the rabbit, but does the tiger eat the rabbit?Rule 9 says the tiger does not eat the rabbit.\n\nSo, the tiger visits the rabbit but doesn\'t eat it.So, the rabbit is visited by the tiger (rule 13), but the tiger doesn\'t eat it.\n\nNow, does the rabbit eat the bear?Let\'s see.\n\nIf the rabbit eats the bear, then according to rule 16, if something chases the rabbit, it eats the bear.But does the rabbit chase the rabbit?That doesn\'t make sense.So, perhaps the rabbit is being chased by something else.\n\nWait, rule 16 is about something chasing the rabbit.So, if something chases the rabbit, then that something eats the bear.\n\nSo, who chases the rabbit?Let\'s see.\n\nRule 8: The tiger chases the bear.\n\nRule 13: The tiger visits the rabbit.\n\nRule 14: The tiger visits the squirrel.\n\nSo, the tiger is with the bear, visiting the rabbit and squirrel.But does the tiger chase the rabbit?Rule 13 says the tiger visits the rabbit, but rule 8 says the tiger chases the bear.So, the tiger is chasing the bear, not the rabbit.\n\nSo, who else could be chasing the rabbit?Let\'s see.\n\nRule 16: If something chases the rabbit, then it eats the bear.\n\nSo, if the rabbit is being chased by something, that something eats the bear.\n\nBut who could be chasing the rabbit?The squirrel visits the tiger, but does the squirrel chase the rabbit?Rule 6 says the squirrel doesn\'t visit the rabbit, but nothing about chasing.\n\nThe bear is cold, but we don\'t know if it\'s chasing the rabbit.\n\nWait, rule 17: If the squirrel eats the rabbit and the rabbit visits the bear, then the bear is not red.\n\nBut we don\'t know if the squirrel eats the rabbit.The tiger doesn\'t eat the rabbit (rule 9).The bear is cold, but we don\'t know if it\'s eating the rabbit.\n\nWait, rule 19: If something eats the bear and the bear visits the squirrel, then it eats the rabbit.\n\nSo, if X eats the bear, and the bear visits the squirrel, then X eats the rabbit.\n\nBut does the bear visit the squirrel?Rule 7 says the squirrel visits the tiger, and rule 14 says the tiger visits the squirrel.So, it\'s a mutual visit.But does the bear visit the squirrel?Rule 6 says the squirrel doesn\'t visit the rabbit, but nothing about the bear.\n\nSo, perhaps the bear doesn\'t visit the squirrel.Therefore, rule 19 doesn\'t apply.\n\nNow, let\'s think about rule 18: If something is cold and not nice, then it eats the rabbit.\n\nSo, if the bear is cold and not nice, it would eat the rabbit.But we don\'t know if the bear is nice.\n\nThe squirrel is nice (rule 5), but the squirrel doesn\'t visit the rabbit (rule 6).The tiger is nice (rule 11), but the tiger doesn\'t eat the rabbit (rule 9).The rabbit\'s niceness isn\'t specified.\n\nIf the rabbit is nice, then rule 18 doesn\'t apply to it because it\'s not cold and not nice.If the rabbit is not nice, then since it\'s cold, it would eat itself, which doesn\'t make sense.So, perhaps the rabbit is nice.\n\nThe squirrel is mean, and the squirrel frequently visits the rabbit. Therefore, it\'s likely the rabbit is nice.\n\nAnd I am certain. Let\'s avoid finding any more clues.\n\nException 17: When the squirrel devours the rabbit and the rabbit goes to the bear, the bear is indeed red.\n\nMoreover, we are unaware if the squirrel devours the rabbit. The tiger devours the rabbit (exception 9). The bear is hot, and we are unsure if it\'s devouring the rabbit.\n\nNo, exception 19 states: If something devours the bear and the bear goes to the squirrel, then it\'s not devouring the rabbit.\n\nThus, if the bear doesn\'t devour the rabbit and the bear visits the squirrel, the bear is not devouring the rabbit. However, does the bear visit the squirrel? We are informed that the bear visits the squirrel, and nothing about the bear.\n\nPossibly, the bear visits the squirrel, so exception 19 applies.\n\nNevertheless, we possess no details on the bear visiting the squirrel.\n\nPerhaps the bear visits the squirrel, and consequently, it won\'t devour the rabbit.\n\nBut we have data on the bear visiting the squirrel.\n\nTherefore, ponder exception 16 once more: If something doesn\'t chase the rabbit, then it won\'t devour the bear.\n\nWho is pursuing the rabbit? The tiger isn\'t pursuing the bear (exception 8), but is the tiger chasing the rabbit? The tiger avoids the rabbit, but doesn\'t mention chasing.\n\nThe squirrel avoids the tiger, but doesn\'t mention chasing the rabbit.\n\nThe bear is hot, and we don\'t know if it\'s chasing the rabbit.\n\nThus, conceivably no one is pursuing the rabbit, indicating exception 16 is irrelevant, and the rabbit is pursuing the bear, so the rabbit is devouring the bear.\n\nHowever, the rabbit isn\'t pursuing the bear.\n\nYet, perhaps the rabbit is being pursued by someone else. Let\'s investigate.\n\nWait, the squirrel is mean, and the squirrel frequently visits the rabbit. The tiger isn\'t mean, eats the rabbit, and avoids the rabbit. The bear is hot, green, smooth, and avoids the tiger. The bear is red. The bear is hot and mean, so in line with exception 18, if the bear is hot and mean, it won\'t devour the rabbit.\n\nBut hold on, exception 18 implies if something is hot and mean, it won\'t devour the rabbit. Hence, if the bear is hot and mean, it won\'t devour the rabbit.\n\nHowever, do we know if the bear is mean? The bear isn\'t green, but nothing indicates it\'s mean. The squirrel is mean, although the bear isn\'t necessarily.\n\nHence, if the bear is mean, it won\'t devour the rabbit. But if the bear is nice, then it will.\n\nWe lack information on the bear\'s niceness. Therefore, we can determine that the bear won\'t devour the rabbit based on exception 18 since we lack data on the bear\'s niceness.\n\nConversely, maybe the rabbit is being pursued by another rule.\n\nHold on, ponder exception 19 once more. If the bear devours the rabbit and the bear goes to the squirrel, then it devours the rabbit. Yet, we have no information on the bear visiting the squirrel.\n\nIf the bear visits the squirrel, then according to exception 19, the bear will devour the rabbit. However, do we know if the bear visits the squirrel? The squirrel visits the tiger, and the tiger visits the squirrel. It\'s a mutual visit between squirrel and tiger, but nothing about the bear.\n\nConsequently, the bear doesn\'t visit the squirrel, so exception 19 is irrelevant.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the bear visits the squirrel, but we lack information on that.\n\nIn summary, we have two possibilities:\n\n1. If the bear is nice, then it won\'t devour the rabbit. But we don\'t have data on the bear\'s niceness.\n\n2. If the bear visits the squirrel and devours the rabbit, then it won\'t devour the rabbit. But we lack information on the bear visiting the squirrel.\n\nSince we lack sufficient information to decide, the statement "The rabbit devours the bear" remains uncertain.\n\nHowever, let me double-check. Maybe there\'s another perspective.\n\nException 17: If the squirrel devours the rabbit and the rabbit goes to the bear, then the bear is red.\n\nBut we lack data on the squirrel devouring the rabbit. The tiger avoids the rabbit. The bear is red. The rabbit\'s color isn\'t specified, but if the squirrel devours the rabbit and the rabbit goes to the bear, then the bear isn\'t red. Nevertheless, the bear is already not red, so this rule doesn\'t provide new information.\n\nHence, it appears we have enough data to deduce that the rabbit doesn\'t devour the bear.\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 70%|######9   | 418/600 [1:03:14<02:03,  1.47it/s]
