e lion.\n18.If the cow is blue, then the cow likes the squirrel.\n19.If the bald eagle sees the lion, then the lion sees the squirrel.\n20.If someone sees the squirrel, then they are green.\nIf someone dislikes the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is not blue.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the bald eagle avoids the lion. From the given facts, point 4 says the bald eagle dislikes the lion. But does disliking the lion mean they avoid each other? Possibly. The context doesn\'t explicitly state that disliking implies avoiding.\n\nLooking at the conditional statements, point 16 says if someone dislikes the bald eagle, they dislike the lion. But that doesn\'t mean they avoid each other. Similarly, point 4 says the bald eagle dislikes the lion, but there\'s no direct information about avoiding.\n\nPoint 19 says if the bald eagle sees the lion, then the lion sees the squirrel. But we don\'t know if the bald eagle sees the lion. Point 20 says if someone sees the squirrel, they are not green. The squirrel is not green (point 12), so someone sees the squirrel, but we don\'t know who that someone is.\n\nPoint 15 is interesting: if someone is not red and avoids the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is not green. But the bald eagle is not blue (point 2), so that condition doesn\'t apply here.\n\nPoint 21 says if someone dislikes the bald eagle, the bald eagle is not blue. But the bald eagle is not blue, so that\'s consistent, but it doesn\'t tell us about avoiding.\n\nPutting it all together, the bald eagle dislikes the lion, but disliking doesn\'t necessarily mean avoiding. There\'s no information that directly links the bald eagle avoiding the lion. Therefore, based on the given information, we can\'t conclude that the bald eagle avoids the lion. It might be true, but we don\'t have enough evidence to confirm it.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
