nd smart (from point 10) implies young (point 12).Young and smart implies white (point 11).But Harry is red, so he can't be white. Contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the only way to avoid contradiction is if Harry isn't blue, but point 6 says if he's kind, he's blue. So he must be blue.\n\nBut then, the conclusion is that Harry is smart because he's red, so the statement \"Harry is not smart\" is false.\n\nWait, but the contradiction arises because of the other points, but maybe the question is only about whether Harry is smart or not, regardless of the contradictions elsewhere.\n\nSo, from point 10, Harry is red, so he's smart. Therefore, the statement \"Harry is not smart\" is false.\n\nSo the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Harry is not smart\" is false because Harry is red, and all red people are smart. Therefore, Harry must be smart.\n\nAnswer: B) False\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. Harry is red (given).\n2. All red people are smart (point 10).\n3. Therefore, Harry is smart.\n4. The statement claims Harry is not smart, which contradicts point 10.\n5. Hence, the statement is false.\n\nAnswer: B) False",
