hing visits the mouse, then the mouse is big.\n- The mouse is visiting the dog, so statement 8 doesn't apply because it's the mouse being visited by the tiger, not the mouse visiting something else.\n\nWait, no. Statement 8 is about the mouse being visited by something. So if the tiger is visiting the mouse, then according to statement 8, the mouse is big.\n\nTherefore, the mouse is big, making the statement \"The mouse is not big\" false.\n\nBut earlier I thought the mouse is not big because it's visiting the dog, but that's incorrect. The correct reasoning is that the mouse is being visited by the tiger, making it big.\n\nSo the correct answer is that the mouse is big, making the statement \"The mouse is not big\" false.\n\nBut wait, I'm getting conflicting conclusions. Let me try to structure it properly.\n\n1. Mouse is green (statement 3).\n2. Mouse visits the dog (statement 12).\n3. Mouse is not visiting the mouse, so statement 8 doesn't apply.\n4. Mouse eats the tiger (statement 2).\n5. Tiger visits the mouse (statement 9).\n6. Tiger is visiting the mouse, so according to statement 8, the mouse is big.\n7. Therefore, the mouse is big, so the statement \"The mouse is not big\" is false.\n\nSo the correct answer is B) False.\n\nAnswer: B) False",
